## Livewire Rules

All rules MANDATORY.

### Component Structure

Use standard class-based Livewire components.

**Properties:**

- Use `public` properties for data binding
- Use `protected` properties for internal state
- Type all properties

**Mounting:**

- Keep `mount()` lightweight
- Initialize complex data only when needed

```php
class CreateUser extends Component
{
    public string $name = '';
    public string $email = '';

    public function save(UserService $service): void
    {
        $this->validate();

        $service->create($this->name, $this->email);

        $this->dispatch('user-created');
        $this->reset();
    }

    public function render(): View
    {
        return view('livewire.create-user');
    }
}
```

### Validation

- Define `$rules` property or `rules()` method
- Use `$this->validate()` before actions

### Blade & Frontend

- Use Alpine.js for client-side interactivity
- Use `@entangle` to share state between Livewire and Alpine
- Use `wire:loading` for visual feedback

```html
<!-- ✅ CORRECT -->
<button wire:click="save" wire:loading.attr="disabled">
    <span wire:loading.remove>Save</span>
    <span wire:loading>Saving...</span>
</button>
```

### Computed Properties

Use `#[Computed]` attribute for expensive queries or derived state.

```php
use Livewire\Attributes\Computed;

#[Computed]
public function posts()
{
    return Post::where('user_id', $this->userId)->get();
}
```

### Optimization

- Use `wire:navigate` for SPA-like transitions
- Use `lazy` loading for heavy components: `<livewire:heavy-component lazy />`
- Avoid passing large objects (Models) to public properties if not needed (use IDs)
